## Install

```sh
npm install {{project_slug}}
```

ESM only: load it with `import`, or with `require()` on a Node version that loads ES modules synchronously. Types are included.

## API

Every export is listed here. A change to this list is a release decision: a new export is a minor release, a removed or renamed one is a major release.

### `slugify(input, options?)`

Turns text into a URL-safe slug. Accents are dropped and anything that isn't a letter or digit separates words.

```ts
import { slugify } from "{{project_slug}}";

slugify("Crème Brûlée, 2 ways!"); // "creme-brulee-2-ways"
slugify("Hello World", { separator: "_", lowercase: false }); // "Hello_World"
```

`SlugifyOptions`:

| Option | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `separator` | `"-"` | One character, not a letter, digit or space, put between words |
| `maxLength` | `80` | Longest slug returned, cut at a word boundary where possible |
| `lowercase` | `true` | Lowercase the result |

Throws `LibraryError` (below). An unknown option key is an error, not ignored.

### `safeSlugify(input, options?)`

The same, returning `Result<string>`: `{ ok: true, value }` or `{ ok: false, error }`, where `error` is the `LibraryError` `slugify` would throw. Anything else still throws.

### `LibraryError` and `isLibraryError(value)`

The one error the package throws on purpose. `error.code` is a `LibraryErrorCode`, `error.field` names the argument or option at fault, never its value.

| Code | When |
| --- | --- |
| `invalid_input` | `input` isn't a string |
| `invalid_option` | an option has a bad value or an unknown key (`field` names it) |
| `empty_result` | the input has no letters or digits |

Use `isLibraryError(error)` rather than `instanceof` when an app may have two copies of the package installed.

